{
  "dom_id": "step-q94828",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{7}^{2})$ with $P(X<6)=0.6736,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6)=0.6736\\,\u21d2\\,P(X\\le 6)=0.6736\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6736 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-\\mu }{7}) & =0.6736 \\\\ \u03a6(\\frac{6-\\mu }{7}) & =0.6736 \\\\ \\frac{6-\\mu }{7} & ={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.6736)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(0.45)=0.6736\\,\u21d2\\,{\u03a6}^{-1}(0.6736)=0.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-\\mu }{7} & =0.45 \\\\ 6-\\mu  & =3.15 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
